{"title":"Isoperimetric Inequality","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Statement\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Statement</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Discrete_Case_(Polygons)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Discrete Case (Polygons)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Proof_for_Rectangles_(Using_AM-GM)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof for Rectangles (Using AM-GM)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Isosurface_Inequality_(3D_Generalization)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Isosurface Inequality (3D Generalization)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Discrete_Case_for_Polyhedra\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Discrete Case for Polyhedra</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Equality_Conditions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Equality Conditions</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Physical_Interpretation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Physical Interpretation</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Applications\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Applications</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Statement\">Statement</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isoperimetric_Inequality&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Statement\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The isoperimetric inequality relates the length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> of a closed curve and the area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> it encloses. In the Euclidean plane, it is stated as:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/b/6ebc4a2e2d04a6d4479473a53b2223c544ac1a9a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[L^2 \\ge 4\\pi A\\]\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" />\nEquality holds if and only if the curve is a circle.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Discrete_Case_.28Polygons.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Discrete_Case_(Polygons)\">Discrete Case (Polygons)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isoperimetric_Inequality&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Discrete Case (Polygons)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For a polygon with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> sides of lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/1/f81465f6b02301397dd62f8fabd0eb65bc53077b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"100\" height=\"11\" />, perimeter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/c/abce1448fc3fe3f17ba04d381bda3854c8bd41a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P = \\sum a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"84\" height=\"31\" />, and area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, the isoperimetric inequality for polygons states:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/d/bdd5ae3cf40bf831fe0ca166b78968ed42e5b3e1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A \\le \\frac{P^2}{4n \\tan(\\frac{\\pi}{n})}\\]\" width=\"115\" height=\"45\" />\nEquality holds if and only if the polygon is a <b>regular <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-gon</b>. This implies two conditions:\n</p>\n<ol><li>(1) The polygon must be equilateral (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/2/d929a7f9f3f49dd6d6d8f390582c4343bc05a2ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1 = a_2 = \\dots = a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"149\" height=\"13\" />).</li>\n<li>(2) The polygon must be equiangular.</li></ol>\n<h2><span id=\"Proof_for_Rectangles_.28Using_AM-GM.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_for_Rectangles_(Using_AM-GM)\">Proof for Rectangles (Using AM-GM)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isoperimetric_Inequality&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Proof for Rectangles (Using AM-GM)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For a rectangle with sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, the perimeter is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/c/82c9c18e55c03dfeff0063198c56f838e3a933ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P = 2(a+b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"103\" height=\"20\" /> and the area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/a/83acfd507d9d6ad549fdf04d305c0a9969345e73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = ab$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" />. \nBy the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AM-GM_Inequality\" title=\"AM-GM Inequality\">AM-GM Inequality</a>:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/5/bd546a96b9fa8b1d77afe09bfc85d1390263ed11.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{a+b}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{ab}\\]\" width=\"98\" height=\"37\" />\nSquaring both sides:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/e/66eb101d38533e1c2b1fafb9bc349ddee003d2bf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{(a+b)^2}{4} \\ge ab\\]\" width=\"105\" height=\"39\" />\nSubstituting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/7/0075d67cef288c00fcd9f11c29a71dfb1af53044.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{(P/2)^2}{4} \\ge A \\implies \\frac{P^2}{16} \\ge A\\]\" width=\"205\" height=\"39\" />\nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/c/39cf3e35a4981583288d2a7c4b34989916fb7360.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> for a rectangle, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/0/88017a2f03da25f8f4bce497a7a4fea84d673392.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4(4)\\tan(\\pi/4) = 16(1) = 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"215\" height=\"20\" />, this matches the discrete formula. Equality holds iff <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/b/5abd2c942ae299fd26e18d0ca5d390a7ca4a8c34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=b$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> (a square).\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Isosurface_Inequality_.283D_Generalization.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Isosurface_Inequality_(3D_Generalization)\">Isosurface Inequality (3D Generalization)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isoperimetric_Inequality&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Isosurface Inequality (3D Generalization)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The isoperimetric principle extends to three dimensions, stating that for a given surface area, the sphere encloses the maximum volume. For a solid with surface area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> and volume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/d/12d58aa29201da09d8e620f8698e3a37547f6b4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/3/053d48e9c624ae464c7509989f504b6f88eb0ee2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S^3 \\ge 36\\pi V^2\\]\" width=\"93\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>This can be rewritten in several elegant forms:\n</p>\n<ul><li><b>Volume-centric form:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/a/70adeeec7439884af6d6cfbe84050c41a6afd0d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V \\le \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\pi}} S^{3/2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"113\" height=\"43\" /></li>\n<li><b>Radius-based comparison:</b> If we let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the radius of a sphere with surface area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/d/efde4a8d482cf66c2c745157e63a3ba358ae76c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S = 4\\pi R^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"80\" height=\"17\" />. Substituting this into the inequality shows <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/2/7a201c103891355ac048ef6d889425044b55aef9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V \\le \\frac{4}{3}\\pi R^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"86\" height=\"38\" />.</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Discrete_Case_for_Polyhedra\">Discrete Case for Polyhedra</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isoperimetric_Inequality&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Discrete Case for Polyhedra\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Similar to the polygon case in 2D, there is a discrete version for polyhedra. For a given surface area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, the volume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/d/12d58aa29201da09d8e620f8698e3a37547f6b4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is maximized when the polyhedron is **regular** (a Platonic Solid).\n</p><p>For a regular polyhedron with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> faces, the relationship is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/5/0956a73f27e1f84ba716b4bf277e49d65a91612b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[V^2 \\le \\frac{S^3}{36n \\tan^2(\\frac{\\pi}{n}) \\sin(\\frac{2\\pi}{n})} \\text{ (approximate form)}\\]\" width=\"346\" height=\"45\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Equality_Conditions\">Equality Conditions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isoperimetric_Inequality&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Equality Conditions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Equality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/a/d7aca3fd335cea89b018a9d909a2eb09a34eec59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S^3 = 36\\pi V^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"97\" height=\"17\" /> holds if and only if the surface is a **perfect sphere**. \n</p>\n<ul><li>In the case of a rectangular parallelepiped (box) with sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc6d9a6172d35127b6cb37ea92192caf7029a54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />, equality in the discrete version <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/9/c19f27ab8e5e98a3a20ae84116c229d2f75aa26d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S^3 \\ge 216 V^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"95\" height=\"19\" /> holds iff <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/6/566b9136144a62fac113ecbc8065793a74123238.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=b=c$\" width=\"74\" height=\"12\" /> (a cube).</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Physical_Interpretation\">Physical Interpretation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isoperimetric_Inequality&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Physical Interpretation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>This inequality explains why:\n</p>\n<ol><li>Raindrops and soap bubbles are spherical (minimizing surface tension/area for a fixed volume).</li>\n<li>Small animals often curl into a ball in winter to minimize surface area and reduce heat loss.</li></ol>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Applications\">Applications</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Isoperimetric_Inequality&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Applications\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><b>Dido's Problem:</b> Finding the maximum area bounded by a line and a curve of fixed length (the solution is a semicircle).</li>\n<li><b>Optimization:</b> Determining the most \"efficient\" shape for packaging or containment.</li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018091945\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.021 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.035 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 214/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6777/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36799:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018091945 and revision id 284384. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}